# ============================================================
# flow/antenna_culled_partner.buda
#
# A segment whose PARTNER is culled out from under it.
#
# This is the shape flow/rv/soc shows mid-flow: a Z_VHV whose
# far leg lands on a keepout, so DetailedNUTS culls every one of
# its bits.  The middle segment's far junction then refers to a
# wire that no longer exists, and on the face of it the whole
# middle segment is metal aimed at nothing.
#
# It is checked in as a GUARD, not as an open defect: on today's
# tree the shape is HANDLED, and this vehicle is what would
# notice if that stopped being true.  `adjust_bit_spans` treats
# an endpoint conn that resolves to no wire for this bit as a
# stale end and retracts to the bit's real attachment (#678, and
# #690 for the busterm-tap variant), so the middle segment
# collapses to its remaining via instead of dangling:
#
#   seg0 bit0  M5 track=183.5  span=[200.0,399.5]
#   seg1 bit0  M4 track=399.5  span=[183.5,183.5]   <- retracted
#   seg2       culled (4 bits unplaced)
#
# So `check_design` reports the REAL problem — 4 unplaced bits and
# the receiver left uncovered — and does NOT report dangling
# metal, because there is none.  A regression in the retraction
# turns seg1 back into 520 units of it.
#
# THE CONSTRUCTION IS THE INTERESTING PART.  A keepout declared
# up front is one the PLANNER sees and routes around, so it can
# never produce this: the planner simply picks another candidate.
# What produces it is the planner committing to ground that only
# turns out blocked later — which is exactly what happens on a
# DEF-imported design, where rv/soc gets its blockages.  Here
# that is arranged deterministically by declaring the keepout
# AFTER `run_planner`, so the planner never saw it and only the
# per-bit keepout cull at DetailedNUTS does.
#
# The vehicle ends DIRTY on purpose (4 unplaced bits): culling a
# segment breaks the route, and that is the point of the setup.
# Do not add it to the QoR corpus.
#
# Run:  bin/buda flow/antenna_culled_partner.buda --no-viz
# ============================================================

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 33.33
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 33.33

corner_margin dx 10 dy 10

add_block drv  100 100  200 200
add_block rcv  700 600  800 700

add_bus b[4] drv.tx rcv.rx

run_bundler strict
generate_topologies

# Candidate 4 is Z_VHV@y400@x190 — V up from drv, H across, V
# down into rcv.  Pinned so the vehicle demonstrates ONE shape;
# without a pin the planner is free to pick an L and there is no
# middle segment to strand.  (The pin is by index, so a change to
# the candidate ORDER moves it — `dump_topologies` after
# `generate_topologies` shows the list.)
select_topology 1 4

run_planner 1
run_nuts

def_track_pattern 4 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4
def_track_pattern 5 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4

# Declared HERE, after the plan is committed: the planner never
# sees it, so seg2 keeps its assignment and only DNUTS's per-bit
# keepout cull acts on it.  Covers seg2's column (x 710) and its
# whole span, so all four of its bits go.
add_keepout 600 380 900 720 5

run_detailed_nuts
dump_topologies --conn
check_design dnuts
report_wirelength
